{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>For</b> — the meaning is: <b>who forms the mountains</b>, that they stand forever. And the meaning: that He is mighty." "<b>And creates the wind</b> — which does not stand in one course, and because of His great might, HaShem shakes and stirs the wind so that what is unseen comes, like standing buildings and trees." "<b>And declares</b> — the meaning is: behold He has declared to you." "<b>What is His thought</b> — what is His will to do, and He will turn dawn into darkness upon you and darkness will return, like: <i>a land of darkness like gloom</i>, and He will tread upon your high places and you will not see Him. And Yefet said: <b>who forms the mountains</b>, which are seen, <b>and creates winds</b> among the nations, <b>and declares to man what is His thought</b> — what he speaks in his heart. And according to the way of the commentators: for HaShem does as He wills a thing and its opposite; therefore if you return to Him — He will turn the evil decrees to good ones."], :book :amos, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {3 "'Yefet' most likely refers to Japheth ben Eli, the 11th-century Karaite exegete. The phrase 'a land of darkness like gloom' alludes toJob or a related wisdom passage. 'And its opposite' renders דבר והפכו hyper-literally."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Amos -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/amos"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:18:07.638541328Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}